(ASK YOUR PDFs | EMBEDDINGS AND SEMANTIC SEARCH) Tj
T*
(An original sample document for the Folio demo collection) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. What an embedding represents) Tj
T*
(An embedding is a numeric representation of an item, such as a sentence or paragraph. A model ) Tj
T*
(learns to map items with related meaning to nearby regions of a vector space. The vector does ) Tj
T*
(not store a short English definition of the passage. It is a machine-readable representation ) Tj
T*
(that makes comparison possible.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For document search, the same embedding model should encode both stored passages and incoming ) Tj
T*
(questions. If a passage says ?the system retrieves relevant context before a response is ) Tj
T*
(composed? and a question asks ?how does RAG find supporting text??, a semantic model may ) Tj
T*
(recognize their relationship even though few words match exactly. The model's training data and ) Tj
T*
(architecture shape what kinds of similarity it captures.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Similarity and nearest neighbors) Tj
T*
(A vector index searches for passage vectors close to a query vector. Cosine similarity compares ) Tj
T*
(the angle between vectors; a smaller angle generally indicates a stronger semantic match. Some ) Tj
T*
(databases report cosine distance instead, where smaller values mean closer vectors. Applications ) Tj
T*
(should confirm how a library defines and returns its scores before interpreting them.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Nearest-neighbor search is a ranking, not a fact check. The first result is merely the most ) Tj
T*
(similar result available in the indexed collection. If every passage is unrelated, one of them ) Tj
T*
(may still rank first. Relevance thresholds, document filters, and a generation instruction to ) Tj
T*
(abstain can reduce unsupported responses, but they require evaluation against real queries.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. Chunk granularity and overlap) Tj
T*
(Passage size affects both retrieval and answer quality. A very short chunk may omit the subject ) Tj
T*
(of a sentence. A very long chunk may contain multiple topics, making its embedding less precise. ) Tj
T*
(Paragraph-level chunks are often a sensible starting point because paragraphs tend to group ) Tj
T*
(related ideas. For long paragraphs, character or token limits provide an upper bound.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Overlapping adjacent chunks preserves context across a boundary. If chunk A ends with a ) Tj
T*
(definition and chunk B begins with its explanation, an overlap gives each retrieved passage a ) Tj
T*
(little shared context. Excessive overlap stores redundant text, increases database size, and may ) Tj
T*
(cause search results to repeat the same passage. Measure chunk length and inspect results ) Tj
T*
(instead of choosing overlap by habit.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Page boundaries have an independent purpose: citations. A chunk that crosses a page boundary ) Tj
T*
(complicates a page-level citation. Keeping chunks inside page boundaries makes it possible to ) Tj
T*
(attach the exact page number at ingestion time. The database can store filename, page, and chunk ) Tj
T*
(order as ordinary metadata fields.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. Vector indexes in practice) Tj
T*
